<p class="lead">You have 47 glowing five-star reviews. Your competitor down the street has 23. You should outrank them, right?</p>
<p class="lead">Not necessarily.</p>
<p>Over the past three years, we have audited more than 1,200 contractor Google Business Profiles across every major trade in the United States. Time and again, we see the same pattern: a company with fewer total reviews but a steady stream of new ones every month consistently outranks a competitor with a higher cumulative count that has gone silent. The difference is not the total number of reviews. It is the speed at which those reviews are coming in.</p>
<p>Welcome to the review velocity effect.</p>
<p>In this study, we analyzed 300+ contractor Google Business Profiles to answer one critical question: <strong>Does review velocity (reviews per month) correlate with local pack rankings?</strong> The answer reshapes how home service businesses should think about reputation management, review generation, and local SEO strategy.</p>
<p>If you have ever wondered whether chasing reviews is worth the effort, whether a sudden spike in reviews helps or hurts, or how your trade compares to others in the review race, this data-driven analysis is for you.</p>
<hr>
<h2>What Is Review Velocity and Why Does It Matter?</h2>
<p>Review velocity is the rate at which your business earns new reviews over a specific time period, typically measured as reviews per month. It is not your lifetime review count. It is not your average star rating. It is the momentum of your reputation, the signal you send to Google that your business is active, relevant, and consistently delivering value to customers.</p>
<p>Think of it like a heartbeat. A steady, rhythmic pulse tells Google your business is alive and thriving. A flatline, even with a strong history, suggests stagnation. And an erratic spike followed by silence looks suspicious.</p>
<p>Google&#39;s local ranking algorithm weighs hundreds of factors, but reviews sit near the top of the list. The search engine wants to surface businesses that are not only highly rated but also currently active. A plumbing company that earned 15 reviews last month is more likely to be actively serving customers than one that earned its last review six months ago, even if the latter has more total reviews.</p>
<p>For home service businesses, review velocity is especially critical because of the nature of the work. Most contractors serve a finite geographic area and compete in a zero-sum local pack where only three businesses appear in the map results. When a homeowner searches &quot;emergency HVAC repair near me&quot; at 2 a.m., Google does not just want the best-rated company. It wants the best-rated company that is actively in business, responding to customers, and generating recent proof of service.</p>
<p>Review velocity provides that proof.</p>
<h3>The Three Components of Review Velocity</h3>
<p>To fully understand review velocity, you need to look at three distinct metrics:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Monthly Review Count:</strong> The raw number of new reviews earned per month. This is the most common measure of velocity and the one we focused on in our study.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Review Consistency:</strong> The regularity of review acquisition. A business that earns 4 reviews every month has better velocity signals than one that earns 12 reviews in January and none for the next three months.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Review Recency:</strong> The freshness of your most recent reviews. Google places significant weight on reviews from the past 30 to 90 days, making recency a critical component of velocity.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Together, these three components create a composite signal that tells Google whether your business is a current, active choice for searchers or a fading memory.</p>
<h3>Why Contractors Cannot Afford to Ignore Velocity</h3>
<p>Home service businesses operate in a unique local SEO environment. Unlike e-commerce or national brands, you are not competing for broad keywords. You are fighting for visibility in a 10-mile radius, often against 20 to 50 direct competitors. In this hyper-local battlefield, small signals matter enormously.</p>
<p>A roofing contractor in Phoenix with 80 total reviews but only 1 new review in the past three months is sending a weak signal. Meanwhile, a competitor with 45 total reviews but 4 new reviews per month is telling Google, &quot;We are busy, we are trusted, and we are the current choice.&quot; That signal often translates directly into higher local pack rankings, more map impressions, and ultimately more phone calls.</p>
<p>The review velocity effect is not theoretical. It is measurable, actionable, and for contractors who understand it, it is a competitive advantage that compounds over time.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Our Methodology: How We Analyzed 300+ Contractor Profiles</h2>
<p>To produce original, contractor-specific data on review velocity and local rankings, we designed a comprehensive correlation study. We did not rely on surveys, opinions, or recycled industry reports. We collected real data from real businesses and analyzed it statistically.</p>
<h3>Sample Selection</h3>
<p>We analyzed <strong>312 contractor Google Business Profiles</strong> across the United States. Our sample was deliberately diverse to ensure the findings apply broadly across the home services industry.</p>
<p><strong>Trade Distribution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>HVAC contractors: 18% (56 profiles)</li>
<li>Plumbing contractors: 16% (50 profiles)</li>
<li>Electrical contractors: 14% (44 profiles)</li>
<li>Roofing contractors: 15% (47 profiles)</li>
<li>Solar installers: 12% (37 profiles)</li>
<li>General contractors: 10% (31 profiles)</li>
<li>Landscaping/hardscaping: 8% (25 profiles)</li>
<li>Pest control: 7% (22 profiles)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Geographic Distribution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Major metros (population 1M+): 40%</li>
<li>Mid-size cities (population 250K-1M): 35%</li>
<li>Smaller markets (population under 250K): 25%</li>
</ul>
<p>We selected businesses that had been active on Google Business Profile for at least 18 months to ensure sufficient historical data. We excluded profiles with fewer than 5 total reviews to avoid skewing the data with brand-new businesses.</p>
<h3>Data Collection</h3>
<p>For each profile, we collected the following data points:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Total Review Count:</strong> Lifetime number of Google reviews</li>
<li><strong>Average Star Rating:</strong> Current overall rating (1.0 to 5.0)</li>
<li><strong>Review Velocity:</strong> Number of new reviews in the past 90 days, divided by 3 to calculate a monthly average</li>
<li><strong>Local Pack Ranking:</strong> Average position in the local pack for the business&#39;s primary service keyword (e.g., &quot;HVAC repair [city]&quot;) over a 30-day period</li>
<li><strong>Review Response Rate:</strong> Percentage of reviews the business owner responded to</li>
<li><strong>Profile Age:</strong> Months since the GBP was verified</li>
<li><strong>Photo Count:</strong> Number of photos uploaded to the profile</li>
<li><strong>Post Frequency:</strong> Average number of GBP posts per month</li>
</ol>
<p>We used a combination of manual auditing and data extraction tools to collect this information during March and April 2025. Rankings were tracked using location-specific searches from IP addresses within each business&#39;s service area to ensure accuracy.</p>
<h3>Analysis Approach</h3>
<p>Our analysis focused on correlation, not causation. We used Pearson correlation coefficients to measure the strength of relationships between variables. Specifically, we examined:</p>
<ul>
<li>Correlation between review velocity and local pack ranking</li>
<li>Correlation between total review count and local pack ranking</li>
<li>Correlation between average star rating and local pack ranking</li>
<li>Comparative strength of each factor as a ranking predictor</li>
<li>Trade-specific variations in velocity and ranking patterns</li>
</ul>
<p>We also performed a multiple regression analysis to isolate the impact of review velocity while controlling for other factors like total review count, profile age, and photo count.</p>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<p>No study is perfect, and we believe in transparency. Our limitations include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Correlation, not causation:</strong> We cannot definitively say that higher velocity causes better rankings, only that the two are strongly associated.</li>
<li><strong>Snapshot in time:</strong> Rankings fluctuate. Our 30-day tracking window provides a reliable snapshot but may not capture seasonal variations.</li>
<li><strong>Geographic bias:</strong> While we covered multiple markets, the majority of our sample came from the Sun Belt and Midwest where home service competition is fiercest.</li>
<li><strong>Google&#39;s black box:</strong> We do not have access to Google&#39;s exact algorithm. Our findings represent observable patterns, not confirmed algorithmic weights.</li>
</ul>
<p>Despite these limitations, the sample size, geographic diversity, and statistical rigor make this one of the most comprehensive contractor-specific review studies available.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Key Findings: What the Data Reveals About Review Velocity</h2>
<p>The results of our analysis were striking. Review velocity emerged as one of the strongest predictors of local pack ranking, often outperforming total review count and even average star rating in correlation strength.</p>
<h3>Finding 1: Review Velocity Correlates More Strongly with Rankings Than Total Review Count</h3>
<p>Our primary finding was clear and statistically significant. <strong>Review velocity showed a correlation coefficient of -0.67 with local pack ranking</strong> (negative because lower ranking numbers mean better positions, i.e., rank 1 is better than rank 3). Total review count showed a correlation of -0.51. Average star rating showed a correlation of -0.38.</p>
<p>In plain terms: the speed at which you earn new reviews is a better predictor of your local pack position than how many reviews you have in total or what your average star rating is.</p>
<p>This finding challenges the conventional wisdom that contractors should focus primarily on accumulating as many reviews as possible. While total count still matters, our data suggests that a business with 50 reviews earning 5 new ones per month will likely outrank a business with 150 reviews earning 1 new one per month.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your business:</strong> Stop obsessing over your lifetime review total. Start obsessing over your monthly review acquisition rate. A consistent 4 to 6 new reviews per month is a stronger ranking signal than a stagnant profile with 200 reviews.</p>
<h3>Finding 2: The &quot;Velocity Threshold&quot; Effect</h3>
<p>Our data revealed a clear threshold effect. Businesses earning <strong>fewer than 2 new reviews per month</strong> showed weak ranking performance regardless of their total review count. The average local pack ranking for this group was 6.8, meaning most were buried below the local pack fold or not appearing at all.</p>
<p>Businesses earning <strong>2 to 4 reviews per month</strong> showed moderate performance with an average ranking of 4.2. They typically appeared in the local pack but rarely in the top 3.</p>
<p>The breakthrough came at <strong>4+ reviews per month</strong>. Businesses in this group averaged a local pack ranking of 2.1, with 68% appearing in the top 3 positions. The difference between 3 reviews per month and 5 reviews per month was the difference between page-one obscurity and map-pack dominance.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your business:</strong> 4 reviews per month is your minimum viable velocity. If you are not consistently hitting this number, you are leaving rankings, visibility, and revenue on the table.</p>
<h3>Finding 3: Review Consistency Beats Review Spikes</h3>
<p>We analyzed profiles that had experienced &quot;review spikes,&quot; defined as earning 10 or more reviews in a single month after a period of low activity. Only 23% of these businesses maintained improved rankings three months after the spike. The rest saw their rankings return to baseline or even decline slightly.</p>
<p>In contrast, businesses with consistent monthly velocity (defined as earning within 25% of their 90-day average each month) showed stable or improving rankings in 79% of cases.</p>
<p>Google appears to value consistency over bursts. A sudden influx of reviews followed by silence looks manipulative, even if the reviews are legitimate. Steady, organic growth signals genuine customer satisfaction and ongoing business activity.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your business:</strong> Do not wait until your review count looks embarrassing and then blast your past customers with review requests. Build a system that generates a steady flow of reviews month after month. Consistency is the compound interest of local SEO.</p>
<h3>Finding 4: Star Rating Matters Less Than You Think (Above 4.0)</h3>
<p>Our data showed that average star rating had the weakest correlation with rankings of the three major review factors. More importantly, the difference in ranking between a 4.2-star business and a 4.8-star business was negligible once velocity was controlled for.</p>
<p>However, there was a cliff effect at ratings below 4.0. Businesses with average ratings below 4.0 showed significantly worse rankings regardless of velocity, suggesting that Google applies a minimum quality threshold.</p>
<p>Above that threshold, velocity and total count are far more influential than whether you are a 4.3 or a 4.7.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your business:</strong> Do not panic if your rating dips from 4.8 to 4.5 after a few negative reviews. Focus on generating more positive reviews to maintain velocity. However, if your rating drops below 4.0, address the underlying service issues immediately, as you are likely being filtered by Google&#39;s quality algorithms.</p>
<h3>Finding 5: Review Response Rate Amplifies Velocity Signals</h3>
<p>As a secondary finding, we discovered that businesses that responded to 80% or more of their reviews saw an additional ranking boost equivalent to approximately 1 additional review per month in velocity.</p>
<p>Google has publicly confirmed that responding to reviews is a best practice, and our data supports the idea that active review management amplifies the positive signals of review velocity.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your business:</strong> Responding to reviews is not just good customer service. It is a ranking factor. Set up alerts and respond to every review within 24 to 48 hours. Thank positive reviewers and address negative ones professionally.</p>
